      "text": "To find the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes:**\n   Since each die has 6 faces, the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n   For all four dice to show the same number, we have 6 possible outcomes (one for each number from 1 to 6). So, there are:\n   \\[\n   6 \\text{ outcomes}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of outcomes where not all four dice show the same number:**\n   This is the total number of possible outcomes minus the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:\n   \\[\n   1296 - 6 = 1290\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability that not all four dice show the same number:**\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{215}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
