      "text": "We will solve this problem step by step.\n\nFirst, determine the total number of outcomes when rolling four fair 6-sided dice. Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of outcomes is:\n\\[\n6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296\n\\]\n\nNext, we want to find the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number. There are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6), and each set of four dice all showing the same number (i.e., 1111, 2222, 3333, 4444, 5555, 6666) is a valid outcome. Therefore, there are 6 such outcomes.\n\nThe number of outcomes where not all four dice show the same number is the total number of outcomes minus the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:\n\\[\n1296 - 6 = 1290\n\\]\n\nThe probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{1290 \\div 6}{1296 \\div 6} = \\frac{215}{216}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
